{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And they became serpents.</b> It does not say ויהיו תנינים but rather לתנינים — to teach that there was no power in the sorcerers to make them actual serpents, only that they appeared as serpents through the art of sorcery and sleight of hand. This expression is like the expression (Joshua 7): ״וימס לבב העם ויהי למים״ — where the intention is not actual water but that their hearts softened like a thing placed in water; and likewise (1 Samuel 25): ״וימת לבו בקרבו והוא היה לאבן״ — where one is not to understand an actual stone. And if you say: but concerning Moses this same expression is used — ״ויהי לתנין״ — and it does not say ויהי תנין, one may explain that this one concerning Moses, which says ״ויהי לתנין,״ is a true coming-into-being; it is the expression of (Genesis 2): ״ויהי האדם לנפש חיה״, spoken of the handiwork of the Holy One, blessed be He, which is a true coming-into-being. But ״ויהיו לתנינים״, said of the sorcerers, is a likeness and a counterfeit coming-into-being. And because the expression in both cases is identical — ״ויהי לתנין״ and ״ויהיו לתנינים״ — lest you understand that the matter of Moses resembles that of the sorcerers, therefore it needed to append to it immediately: ״ויבלע מטה אהרן את מטותם.״ And behold, this is a proof that Aaron's staff, after it had been an actual serpent, swallowed their staffs; and Scripture thereby teaches you that it reverted to an actual serpent, but those of the sorcerers were not actual serpents. From this it says את מטותם and does not say את תניניהם. And furthermore, it did not mention concerning the sorcerers that they did so לפני פרעה ולפני עבדיו as it mentioned concerning Moses — for all their deeds are counterfeit, having no truth in them. Or, if we say that there was power in the sorcerers to make actual serpents through sorcery, as our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'Why are they called מכשפים? Because they contradict the entourage on high' — this too is possible, and what it says, ״ויהיו לתנינים,״ would mean actual serpents, and it needed to say לתנינים with the lamed because the word ויהיו refers back to the staffs — that is, ויהיו המטות לתנינים, the staffs became serpents; and likewise with Moses: ״ויהי לתנין״ — the staff became a serpent; and likewise the word of HaShem (Exodus 7): ״יהי לתנין״ — your staff shall become a serpent; and the deed of the sorcerers and the deed of Moses are all equal in actual serpents. But the word ״ויבלע״ is the superiority and the overpowering of strength that Aaron had over the wisdom of the sorcerers — whether the swallower be a staff, as the Sages of blessed memory expounded, or whether it be a serpent, or whether their deed be mere illusion. And the upshot of the matter is that the word בלהטיהם, said of the sorcerers, testifies to the refutation of their wisdom, which is not truth but only counterfeit and trickery; and the word ״ויבלע״ testifies to the wisdom of Moses and Aaron, which is true and overpowers the wisdom of the sorcerers. And the verse ״ויבלע״ is a witness, for the inanimate swallowed the inanimate or the living — and so we find in the matter of Korah, that the inanimate swallowed the living and the inanimate, as it says (Numbers 16): ״ותפתח הארץ את פיה ותבלע אותם ואת בתיהם״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:54.785841811Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}